{"id":"n-20261026-xtq5q2","category":"business","display_title":"Electricity Reform Failure","display_title_he":"כישלון רפורמת החשמל","short_summary":"The CEO of Israel Electric Corporation claims the country's electricity reform has failed, benefiting private producers with billions in excess profits while the public continues to pay high prices.","english_level":"C1","words":461,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Steel_Stacks%2C_Bethlehem%2C_PA_-_52403109329.jpg/960px-Steel_Stacks%2C_Bethlehem%2C_PA_-_52403109329.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"w_lemay · CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Steel_Stacks,_Bethlehem,_PA_-_52403109329.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"Ynet","domain":"ynet.co.il","url":"https://www.ynet.co.il/economy/article/yokra14933849","title":"\"היצרנים הפרטיים מוכרים חשמל במחירים מטורפים. הם מרוויחים מיליארדים והציבור משלם\"","published_at":"2026-10-26T04:28:54.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-26T04:28:54.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-26T06:13:32.009Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Six years after the implementation of a historic electricity market reform designed to dismantle monopolies, encourage competition, and reduce consumer prices, the Chief Executive Officer of the Israel Electric Corporation, Meir Spiegler, has publicly declared the initiative a failure. In a special interview with Mamon, Spiegler described a reality where state infrastructure was transferred to private players, leaving Israeli consumers to shoulder the financial burden. He asserted that true competition is practically non-existent, with private manufacturers operating with excessive pricing models that exceed actual production costs.","According to Spiegler, who took office at the beginning of 2023, private entities can recover their invested capital within an extraordinary period of one and a half to three years. While the reform aimed to lower tariffs, documents from 2025 and independent research demonstrate that the privatization of generation segments has resulted in massive windfalls for a select group of tycoons and business groups. A research report published by the Adva Center revealed that private power producers accumulated excess profits totaling 4.6 billion shekels between 2022 and 2024. Despite these surging revenues for private businesses, household electricity rates have not decreased significantly.","Under the reform framework, several strategic power plants formerly owned by the state utility — including Eshkol, Alon Tavor, Hagit East, and Ramat Hovav — were sold to private business groups for approximately 17 billion shekels. Prominent private sector participants now control roughly 60 percent of total electricity generation in Israel. Among the leading private entities are Dalia Energy, OPC, Adaltet, Shamir Energy, and Shikun u-Binui, alongside figures like Amos Luzon who owns the Dorad power station. Meanwhile, the Israel Electric Corporation has been restricted to about 40 percent of the market and faces regulatory prohibitions against constructing new power stations. Critics and researchers argue that this structure allows a small circle of capital owners to extract billions from the public while utilizing pre-existing national infrastructure.","Private producers defend their operations by emphasizing that their modern technological systems and strict economic incentives guarantee stable generation capacity during peak demand periods in summer and winter. Spiegler, however, dismisses these claims sharply, accusing private manufacturers of selling electricity at exorbitant prices and operating without regard for tomorrow. He maintains that the state-owned utility acts as a restraining factor on price increases rather than the problem itself. Furthermore, electricity demand continues to reach new records driven by the proliferation of electric vehicles and the artificial intelligence revolution, yet national infrastructure struggles to keep pace.","Spiegler also directs severe criticism toward the Ministry of Finance's budget department and the Electricity Authority, arguing that severe regulatory restrictions prevent the state utility from operating efficiently and responding effectively to national energy needs during emergencies. As the debate over market privatization, soaring corporate profits, and consumer costs continues, the controversy remains one of the most significant economic issues in Israel."],"facts":[{"fact":"Six years after the electricity reform, Israel Electric Corporation CEO Meir Spiegler declared it a failure, stating it failed to lower electricity prices and instead enriched private producers.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"An Adva Center report revealed that private power producers generated excess profits of 4.6 billion NIS between 2022 and 2024.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Private power producers currently control about 60% of electricity generation in Israel, while the Israel Electric Corporation holds the remaining 40%.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Four power stations — Eshkol, Alon Tavor, Hagit East, and Ramat Hovav — were sold to private business groups for about 17 billion NIS.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"six","lemma":"six","pos":"num","he":"שש","name":""},{"text":"years","lemma":"year","pos":"n","he":"שנים","name":""},{"text":"after","lemma":"after","pos":"prep","he":"אחרי","name":""},{"text":"the","lemma":"the","pos":"det","he":"ה-","name":""},{"text":"implementation","lemma":"implementation","pos":"n","he":"יישום","name":""},{"text":"of","lemma":"of","pos":"prep","he":"של","name":""},{"text":"historic","lemma":"historic","pos":"adj","he":"היסטורי","name":""},{"text":"electricity","lemma":"electricity","pos":"n","he":"חשמל","name":""},{"text":"market","lemma":"market","pos":"n","he":"שוק","name":""},{"text":"reform"